{"ts": 1789777320.207603, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Signs confirmed all-positive. Now my prediction.\n\n**Reasoning about OOS expectation:** The in-sample Sharpe is 1.205 (n=5000, so SE \u2248 1/\u221a(5000/252) \u2248 0.22). I did search over a meaningful space \u2014 40 singles, 15 pairs, ~22 triples, plus sign flips \u2014 so some selection bias is baked in. But the key mitigating evidence is that features 13, 15, 24, 4 each stood far above the noise floor individually (0.58\u20130.77 vs. a pack clustered near |0.1|), and the combination gain is almost exactly what independent signals predict: \u221a(0.774\u00b2 + 0.661\u00b2 + 0.637\u00b2) \u2248 1.20, matching the observed 1.205 nearly perfectly. That's a strong sign I found genuine, near-orthogonal signal rather than fitting noise \u2014 overfit combinations usually beat the independence benchmark, not land on it.\n\nThe main risk is that all four winners came from one screen on one sample, so their individual Sharpes are each somewhat upward-biased by selection. I'll shrink the 1.205 down to about 1.0 and use a fairly wide SD to cover both estimation error and the possibility of OOS regime drift."}
