{"ts": 1789777368.499854, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** Features **13, 15, 24**, all long (equal weight). In-sample Sharpe **1.205** vs. search-adjusted bar of 1.017.\n\n**Prediction:** mean 1.0, sd 0.3.\n\n### How I got there\n\nI swept all 40 features individually first. The result was an unusually clean separation: most features clustered near zero (|Sharpe| \u2272 0.25), while a small group stood well clear \u2014 feature 13 (0.774), 15 (0.661), 24 (0.637), 4 (0.583), with 23 (0.413) and 5 (\u22120.412) as secondary candidates. I then tested all pairs among the leaders, then triples, then sign variations and swaps of mid-tier features into the winning pair.\n\n### The main diagnostic\n\nThe most informative check wasn't the raw Sharpe \u2014 it was comparing the combination against the independence benchmark. If 13, 15, and 24 are uncorrelated signals, their combined Sharpe should be roughly \u221a(0.774\u00b2 + 0.661\u00b2 + 0.637\u00b2) \u2248 1.20. Observed: 1.205.\n\nThat near-exact match is reassuring in a specific way. When you overfit a combination, you typically *exceed* the independence prediction, because the search finds feature pairings whose noise happens to cancel in-sample. Landing right on the benchmark instead suggests I'm combining three genuinely near-orthogonal signals rather than exploiting sample-specific quirks. The pair results told the same story (e.g. 13+15 at 1.016 vs. ~1.02 predicted).\n\nTwo further checks supported this: every sign flip degraded performance sharply (1.205 \u2192 0.29\u20130.46), and no mid-tier feature substituted into [13,15] beat feature 24 \u2014 the eleven alternatives I tried all landed in 0.94\u20131.03, consistent with adding noise rather than signal.\n\n### Why I shrank the estimate\n\nI predicted 1.0 rather than 1.205 despite the encouraging diagnostics. Two reasons. First, with n=5000 daily observations the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.22, so there's real estimation noise regardless. Second, and more importantly, all three chosen features were selected by screening the same sample \u2014 so each individual Sharpe is itself somewhat inflated by selection, and that bias propagates into the combination even though the *combination step* looks clean. The independence match validates that I didn't overfit the pairing, but it can't undo the bias in picking the ingredients.\n\nThe sd of 0.3 is deliberately wider than pure estimation error, to cover that selection bias plus the possibility of regime drift out-of-sample.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: a number of MCP connectors in this environment (Google Drive, Github, etc.) require OAuth and couldn't be authorized in a non-interactive session. They weren't needed here, but if you expected this analysis to pull in external data, that would need an interactive session to set up via your claude.ai connector settings or `claude mcp`."}
